WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:08.010
We need to know that
the things we're producing

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:12.030
are safe, are actually
giving us the effect

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:14.730
that we are intending
to create, as well

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:17.200
as not causing any harm.

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:19.200
I think there's a
lot of hope and promise

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:23.280
to having standardized tools
that are used across pharma.

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:25.470
The open source
mindset allows you to ask

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:26.610
all kinds of possibilities.

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:28.410
You can go right back
and, if you're not sure

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:29.970
if something's right, you
can go and explore it.

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:31.970
I think there's a lot
of value in open source,

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:35.070
because that pipeline,
although somewhat

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:37.860
idiosyncratic and unique across
different pharma companies,

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:40.470
at the end of the
day is standardized.

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:43.200
You can get people who have
both industry experience,

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:46.650
they understand what it is
to build an atom data set.

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:48.990
They understand that
process, and so they

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:50.500
have the right skills.

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:52.230
They're just a lot
more transferable.

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:56.490
So I use Posit packages almost
every day when I'm working,

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:58.470
and it makes it
just incredibly easy

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:02.070
to build a package that is
quality, that it's tested.

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:03.880
As a statistician
for instance,

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:05.880
being able to have an
open source package that's

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:08.490
already available greatly
increases how quickly

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:10.985
you can try that method out.

00:01:10.985 --> 00:01:12.360
Our submission
process is still

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.235
based around, essentially,
paper submissions.

00:01:14.235 --> 00:01:17.400
We've made the paper into PDFs,
but it's pretty much the same

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:19.200
as it was 20 or 30 years ago.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:21.930
By submitting to regulators
with open source software,

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:24.760
they can immediately
access that same code.

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:26.430
They can run it on
their own machines,

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:30.050
and verify that they are getting
the same results that we are.

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:32.850
We give them a
lot of code in order

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:35.640
to show, like, this is
all of the things we did.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:39.510
And when it's in an R
package, it's lovely.

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:40.980
It's easy, that's portable.

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:44.080
It's open source, we don't
even have to port it anywhere.

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:45.400
It's already out there.

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:47.820
I think the possibility
now with open source,

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:49.320
and particularly
something like R,

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:51.510
where you've got
a tool like Shiny,

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:55.000
is you can start to deliver that
submission in a different way.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:59.100
I think we'll be sharing
the data directly

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:00.450
with the regulators in future.

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:02.490
We'll be worried less
about how we format that

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:04.920
and get it to look really
nice on a piece of paper.

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:07.530
And I hope that we'll
be able to deliver

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:11.719
these kind of interactive apps
to regulators in the future.

